\chapter{The domain: <one line naming the world this system lives in>}

<Describe the domain as you would to a smart colleague new to it. What are the
actors, the objects, the events? What is the world this program lives in,
before any technology is chosen?>

\section{Purpose}

<What this system is for, stated as the outcome its reader gets — not the
mechanism. One paragraph. If you cannot say it in one paragraph, the domain is
still two domains.>

\section{The problem}

<The concrete problem this solves. What is done today, why it hurts, and what
``solved'' looks like.>

\section{Scope}

<What is in, and — just as important — what is deliberately OUT. A domain with
no edges cannot be framed, and a scope with no exclusions is a wish. Name the
things a reader might reasonably expect here that this system will not do.>
